{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Each according to the gift of his hand, according to the blessing of HaShem your Elohim that He has given you.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 8b) learned from this verse that one who has abundant possessions brings abundant burnt offerings, and if he has many eaters — that is, his children and the members of his household, who are called 'the blessing of HaShem,' as it is said (Psalms 128:3–4): ״Your children like olive shoots around your table; behold, for thus is the man who fears HaShem blessed״ — therefore it first says, concerning the burnt offerings that depend on possessions, whether many or few: ״each according to the gift of his hand״ — that is, according to his possessions, according to the generosity of his hand and his wealth. And afterward it says, concerning the peace offerings that depend on having many eaters: ״according to the blessing of HaShem your Elohim that He has given you״ — for this depends on what HaShem has blessed you and multiplied you." "And since throughout the entire parashah Moses speaks in direct address to Israel, he should have said ״according to the gift of your hand,״ yet he said ״his hand״ in the third person. And perhaps ״his hand״ refers to the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (I Chronicles 29:14): ״For all is from You, and from Your hand we have given to You.״ And he used this expression in connection with burnt offerings, which are entirely consumed on high, lest a person begrudge giving so much of his own to the One above. Therefore he said that it is not from his own that he gives, but from what he received from the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what it says: ״according to the gift of His hand״ — of the Holy One, blessed be He: if from His hand, may He be blessed, abundant possessions were given to him, then he in turn gives of it and brings abundant burnt offerings. And it may also be that 'many eaters' means the stranger and the orphan and the widow, etc., and for their sake he brings many peace offerings — and let your eye not be evil when you give to them, but rather ״according to the blessing of HaShem your Elohim that He has given you״ — and it is not from your own that you give, but from that of the Holy One, blessed be He."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:37.025409479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}